    "content": " The Ivy League consists of eight prestigious, private research universities in the Northeastern U.S. known for academic excellence, selectivity, and historic reputation: Harvard University, Yale University, Princeton University, Columbia University, Brown University, Dartmouth College, the University of Pennsylvania, and Cornell University. Originally an athletic conference formed in 1954, they are now synonymous with elite higher education and have very low admission rates. The Eight Ivy League Schools Brown University (Providence, RI): Known for its \"Open Curriculum\". Columbia University (New York, NY): Known for its core curriculum and NYC location. Cornell University (Ithaca, NY): Known for having the largest and most diverse undergraduate programs. Dartmouth College (Hanover, NH): Known for a strong undergraduate focus and unique D-Plan. Harvard University (Cambridge, MA): Known as the oldest institution of higher education in the US. Princeton University (Princeton, NJ): Known for its focus on undergraduate teaching. University of Pennsylvania (Philadelphia, PA): Known for its renowned Wharton School. Yale University (New Haven, CT): Known for its drama and law schools. Quick Facts About Ivy League Schools Location: All are located in the Northeastern United States (New York, Massachusetts, New Jersey, Pennsylvania, Rhode Island, New Hampshire). Selectivity: As of 2025, admission rates are extremely competitive, with many under 6%. History: Seven of the eight were founded during the Colonial era, with Cornell founded shortly after the Civil War. Costs: While expensive, many offer high-end financial aid, with sticker prices (tuition and fees) roughly around $80,000 to $95,000+ annually. Common Misconceptions Stanford University, MIT, and Duke University are not in the Ivy League, although they are similarly selective. The term \"Ivy League\" is based on athletic conference membership (NCAA Division I) and historical tradition, not just academic ranking.",
